00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:44.850
- Atlanta Braves, the closest one.

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:46.723
There's been talk of
Charlotte getting a team,

00:09:46.723 --> 00:09:48.290
that's right near our house.

00:09:48.290 --> 00:09:49.700
So if that happened I would just--

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:50.533
- You'd lose your shit.

00:09:50.533 --> 00:09:51.930
- It would be insane.

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:53.390
I don't know what I'd do with my career

00:09:53.390 --> 00:09:55.010
that I've got going now in NASCAR

00:09:55.010 --> 00:09:56.300
but man it would be big.

00:09:56.300 --> 00:09:57.550
My wife wouldn't like it.

00:10:00.126 --> 00:10:01.990
- Let's go into origin stories.

00:10:01.990 --> 00:10:03.120
Let's paint a picture for everybody.

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:04.740
Let's do a two or three
minute, four minute,

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:06.230
who are you, where were you born,

00:10:06.230 --> 00:10:07.580
how'd you get into what you're into,

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:09.450
what are you into, blah blah blah.

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:13.270
- All right, I was born
in Indianapolis, Indiana,

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:18.270
so I've been around racing
a good portion of my life.

00:10:19.039 --> 00:10:21.600
It's not NASCAR but I've
been going to Indy 500

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:26.600
my whole life and I went
on to work in television.

00:10:28.030 --> 00:10:30.330
I went to American University,
I always knew I wanted

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:31.300
to work in television.

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:34.100
Never knew exactly which
path I wanted to take

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:35.900
but I always said I wanted
to be the next Oprah,

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:38.190
that was always my big
thing when I was growing up

00:10:38.190 --> 00:10:41.040
and someone actually just
commented on my photo today like,

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:44.130
Charly or Oprah, and I
was like, best compliment

00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:45.820
I've ever gotten in my
life and of course now

00:10:45.820 --> 00:10:47.170
it's on an Instagram photo.

00:10:48.193 --> 00:10:49.770
- Do you want to hear a really fun fact?

00:10:49.770 --> 00:10:50.603
- [Charly] Yeah.

00:10:50.603 --> 00:10:53.200
- You should go look at Oprah's
first Instagram photo ever.

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:54.033
- Really.

00:10:54.033 --> 00:10:56.650
- I'll just leave it at that, keep going.

00:10:56.650 --> 00:10:59.490
- So I graduated with a
Broadcast Journalism degree

00:10:59.490 --> 00:11:00.490
from American.

00:11:00.490 --> 00:11:02.820
- Were you out of control in college

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:04.850
or were you like super focused.

00:11:04.850 --> 00:11:07.020
- I was pretty focused.

00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:10.150
I was in a sorority, and
we had our parties here,

00:11:10.150 --> 00:11:12.850
you know like Chi Omegas
do not drink, do not drink.

00:11:12.850 --> 00:11:14.343
Whatever, we drank.

00:11:15.231 --> 00:11:19.860
Honestly I had a good
time but I'm super type A,

00:11:19.860 --> 00:11:21.630
to a fault sometimes.

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:24.142
3.95 GPA graduated with.

00:11:24.142 --> 00:11:25.230
- Like that boy's cute but
I'm gonna be the next Oprah,

00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:26.743
fuck you Johnny.

00:11:26.743 --> 00:11:29.670
- I tried to skip class once
in college, I freaked out,

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:33.000
I ran to class, no
attendance was even taken.

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:34.223
That's how I was.

00:11:35.607 --> 00:11:37.360
Went to Huntington, West
Virginia for my first job,

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:40.430
one man band reporter,
carried all my shit with me,

00:11:40.430 --> 00:11:42.420
went and interviewed
people about overdosing

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:45.010
on bath salts, it was
very interesting terrain

00:11:45.010 --> 00:11:46.664
in West Virginia.

00:11:46.664 --> 00:11:50.260
Went on to Kansas City, Missouri
still reporting news there,

00:11:50.260 --> 00:11:52.540
went back to Indianapolis,
my home town, that's when

00:11:52.540 --> 00:11:54.838
I got into sports, sports
anchor, sports reporter.

00:11:54.838 --> 00:11:58.840
And then from there, WWE saw
my work, asked me to audition.

00:11:58.840 --> 00:12:00.150
- On a local network?

00:12:00.150 --> 00:12:02.490
- Yeah, I was working for
the FOX station there.

00:12:02.490 --> 00:12:03.650
- Doing sports reporter.
- Yeah.

00:12:03.650 --> 00:12:06.550
- Like the Colts are playing
the Browns this weekend.

00:12:06.550 --> 00:12:07.860
- Exactly.
- Okay.

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:11.810
- And WWE, actually the way
it happened, I was back stage

00:12:11.810 --> 00:12:15.029
at SmackDown, it was in
Indianapolis, and I ran into

00:12:15.029 --> 00:12:18.543
the head of talent relations,
his name was Mark Corono.

00:12:19.780 --> 00:12:22.120
At this point my contract
with Indianapolis had

00:12:22.120 --> 00:12:23.743
just expired, I didn't want to renew it.

00:12:23.743 --> 00:12:28.140
I said, "Hey, I recoginize you
from the show Total Divas,"

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:31.107
which is that reality show,
I said, "I'm a sports caster,

00:12:31.107 --> 00:12:33.050
"would you mind taking a look at my work?"

00:12:33.050 --> 00:12:35.730
He said, sure, didn't
really follow up with me,

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:37.830
so I reached out again a few months later.

00:12:37.830 --> 00:12:39.690
He's like, you're good, I like you,

00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:41.020
I'm going to pass this along.

00:12:41.020 --> 00:12:42.870
So he passed it along to Michael Cole,

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:44.920
who I know you know who he is,

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:46.770
and from there they had me come audition,

00:12:46.770 --> 00:12:50.150
and the rest is history, here I am.

00:12:50.150 --> 00:12:53.720
- So did you grow up
watching wrestling at all?

00:12:53.720 --> 00:12:56.410
- I was a fan at the
beginning of middle school,

00:12:56.410 --> 00:12:59.043
so sixth, seventh grade,
height of the Attitude Era.

00:12:59.043 --> 00:13:00.360
- Got it, so the Stone Cold,

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:02.510
like, everybody cared--
- Everybody loved it.

00:13:02.510 --> 00:13:04.535
- So who were your favorites
then, like Mankind?

00:13:04.535 --> 00:13:07.190
- The Rock, I'm still
obsessed with The Rock.

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:08.050
Like I secretly--

00:13:08.050 --> 00:13:09.510
- Because he's secretly Oprah.

00:13:09.510 --> 00:13:10.343
- Oh he's great.

00:13:10.343 --> 00:13:12.810
- You know The Rock is
actually Oprah, right?

00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:16.570
- Oh yeah, he's fantastic,
so still love him.

00:13:16.570 --> 00:13:18.189
- Yeah, he's amazing.

00:13:18.189 --> 00:13:19.200
What's your story brother?

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:20.470
Lions fan from the get.

00:13:20.470 --> 00:13:22.830
- Yeah, Lions fan from the start.

00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:24.880
I have kind of an odd
story for racing, at least,

00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:25.850
from where I'm at.

00:13:25.850 --> 00:13:26.840
I started when I was seven,

00:13:26.840 --> 00:13:28.385
so I started very young in go-karts.

00:13:28.385 --> 00:13:32.073
I started moving up through
the ranks after that.

00:13:32.073 --> 00:13:33.930
- Why did that happen?
- Why did I start racing?

00:13:33.930 --> 00:13:36.100
- Was your family into it
or did you stumble into it?

00:13:36.100 --> 00:13:39.040
- No, I more so stumbled into it.

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:42.103
My family never raced, but
I was always a fan of cars.

00:13:42.103 --> 00:13:45.612
My mom actually got me into
it, got me started in it.

00:13:45.612 --> 00:13:47.142
- What, she wanted you out of the house?

00:13:47.142 --> 00:13:49.710
- I think so, I wasn't
good at any other sports

00:13:49.710 --> 00:13:51.575
and I didn't have any other hobbies.

00:13:51.575 --> 00:13:52.984
- She's like, get the fuck out of here.

00:13:52.984 --> 00:13:54.308
- Like, go do this instead.

00:13:54.308 --> 00:13:56.569
- Did it click right away?

00:13:56.569 --> 00:13:59.660
- Like was I good right
away, or did I like it?

00:13:59.660 --> 00:14:00.493
- How about both?

00:14:00.493 --> 00:14:02.267
- Immediately I liked it
right away, I loved it.

00:14:02.267 --> 00:14:05.855
And I guess I was decent,
we won some races.

00:14:05.855 --> 00:14:09.530
From there I started moving
up into some lower stuff

00:14:09.530 --> 00:14:11.700
outside of NASCAR, late
models, they call them,

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:13.159
around that area.

00:14:13.159 --> 00:14:16.430
I was racing there until I was about 16

00:14:16.430 --> 00:14:19.540
and I got an opportunity,
actually from Kyle.

00:14:19.540 --> 00:14:20.523
I was racing against him and he came down

00:14:20.523 --> 00:14:22.430
and raced one of those races.

00:14:22.430 --> 00:14:24.470
You know there's no rules
in racing, you can go back,

00:14:24.470 --> 00:14:26.020
run whatever race you want.

00:14:26.020 --> 00:14:27.220
- Why do guys do that?

00:14:27.220 --> 00:14:28.800
Is it to work on something?

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:29.840
Almost like a triple A thing

00:14:29.840 --> 00:14:31.800
or giving love to the community,

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:33.763
or it's a mix of many things?

00:14:34.610 --> 00:14:38.250
- That type of race you're
running is very different

00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:40.163
car than NASCAR.

00:14:41.077 --> 00:14:41.910
- Sometimes they miss it and like it?

00:14:41.910 --> 00:14:44.463
- I think it's kind of a
respect back to the sport

00:14:44.463 --> 00:14:47.250
because it's still not easy
to go back and beat those guys

00:14:47.250 --> 00:14:50.870
who run that car every single
weekend at that same track.

00:14:50.870 --> 00:14:51.703
- That makes sense.

00:14:51.703 --> 00:14:52.843
- And it's a fun thing too

00:14:52.843 --> 00:14:55.520
because it gets back
to your initial roots.

00:14:55.520 --> 00:14:57.710
- So when Kyle comes and
visits and he's already

00:14:57.710 --> 00:14:59.380
in the big series, like
is that fun, you're like

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:01.380
oh shit, yeah, I wanna kill him.

00:15:01.380 --> 00:15:04.313
- Yeah, it's a big deal, you want to win.

00:15:04.313 --> 00:15:07.560
- It's like when Kershaw gets
hurt and does a triple A start

00:15:07.560 --> 00:15:09.300
in New Mexico and
everyone's like holy shit.

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:11.443
I love that, I love that
part of triple A baseball

00:15:11.443 --> 00:15:13.810
or double A baseball, like
when it's a small town

00:15:13.810 --> 00:15:16.741
or what have you, and a big
league star has to come through

00:15:16.741 --> 00:15:18.663
just for a rehab start.

00:15:19.957 --> 00:15:22.140
So, what, you raced in a race with him

00:15:22.140 --> 00:15:23.320
and he just saw the talent?

00:15:23.320 --> 00:15:25.882
- We raced at the end and
I ended up beating him

00:15:25.882 --> 00:15:28.700
in that race and he
gave me an opportunity,

00:15:28.700 --> 00:15:31.385
that was 2012, so in
2013 to drive his truck,

00:15:31.385 --> 00:15:32.814
he has a truck team there.

00:15:32.814 --> 00:15:36.340
I started racing for him
and we won a race in 2013

00:15:37.400 --> 00:15:39.871
and he gave me more
opportunities and we kept winning

00:15:39.871 --> 00:15:41.940
and just moved into a role

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:44.480
over at Joe Gibbs Racing after that.

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:46.140
I mean, there's a lot to
it, I could talk about it--

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:49.277
- I'm sure, but the origin
story is meeting him in a race

00:15:49.277 --> 00:15:53.013
in that series opened up
doors, you kept performing,

00:15:53.013 --> 00:15:55.000
you're on the big show.

00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:56.110
And how is the big show?

00:15:56.110 --> 00:15:58.170
- It's tough, I mean, it's hard.

00:15:58.170 --> 00:16:01.340
It's fun, I love it, but it's tough.

00:16:01.340 --> 00:16:03.870
- Because the transition
to the car is so different?

00:16:03.870 --> 00:16:06.214
- The car and the competition.

00:16:06.214 --> 00:16:07.950
The competition level is
very very high in Cup.

00:16:07.950 --> 00:16:09.870
I mean, just everybody is really good.

00:16:09.870 --> 00:16:12.800
From 1st to 40th, everybody's pretty good.

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:14.582
The thing about cup is at
one point I would say--

00:16:14.582 --> 00:16:15.750
- Have you won a race there yet?

00:16:15.750 --> 00:16:17.580
- One, yeah, just this year.

00:16:17.580 --> 00:16:19.030
- Did you fucking freak your shit?

00:16:19.030 --> 00:16:21.067
- I did, yeah, I was losin' it.

00:16:21.067 --> 00:16:22.180
- Was that crazy?

00:16:22.180 --> 00:16:23.423
- Oh yeah, it was nuts.

00:16:23.423 --> 00:16:24.400
- Was that the goal this year, like,

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:25.730
I gotta get a win on the board?

00:16:25.730 --> 00:16:26.563
- Yeah, for me.

00:16:26.563 --> 00:16:27.720
I wanted to last year.

00:16:27.720 --> 00:16:29.750
We had an opportunity to
win last year a couple times

00:16:29.750 --> 00:16:31.973
and we didn't really execute on it.

00:16:31.973 --> 00:16:35.120
Kyle actually spoiled one of them for me.

00:16:35.120 --> 00:16:36.653
- He seems like a real dick.

00:16:38.230 --> 00:16:40.500
I love it, I love how
competitive he is, actually.

00:16:40.500 --> 00:16:42.620
- He came up and drove by
me with about 50 laps to go,

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:43.820
just kind of, deuces--

00:16:43.820 --> 00:16:44.910
- Peace out.

00:16:44.910 --> 00:16:45.960
- Yeah, I ran second.

00:16:46.870 --> 00:16:49.135
Yeah, I mean, that's a big
goal there, to get a win.

00:16:49.135 --> 00:16:50.870
You want to be a winner there, at the Cup.

00:16:50.870 --> 00:16:52.360
- Good for you, what other
interests do you have,

00:16:53.590 --> 00:16:56.828
outside of motorcycles and
cars, out side of that niche,

00:16:56.828 --> 00:17:01.460
what else, what grabs at
you, cooking, TV shows?

00:17:01.460 --> 00:17:03.364
- I wish I was into cooking,
I wish I was good at it.

00:17:03.364 --> 00:17:07.960
I love to golf, golf, scuba
dive, I'm a scuba diver,

00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:10.570
I haven't been as much
but I like to scuba dive.

00:17:10.570 --> 00:17:15.570
I'm a big outdoors fan, I love
being outside doing whatever.

00:17:15.730 --> 00:17:17.150
- Love it, my man?

00:17:17.150 --> 00:17:19.580
- For me, I grew up in a racing family.

00:17:19.580 --> 00:17:22.290
My grandfather is Richard
Childress, who owned

00:17:22.290 --> 00:17:24.660
Dale Earnhardt's number three car,

00:17:24.660 --> 00:17:26.330
so he's won seven championships

00:17:26.330 --> 00:17:28.750
with Dale Earnhardt and then championships

00:17:28.750 --> 00:17:30.150
through the Xfinity Series.

00:17:30.150 --> 00:17:33.150
- You grew up in, for a
lot of people listening

00:17:33.150 --> 00:17:35.390
who don't know the sport, you
kind of grew up in royalty,

00:17:35.390 --> 00:17:37.615
not even like in a family.

00:17:37.615 --> 00:17:41.091
That must be stupid pressure.

00:17:41.091 --> 00:17:44.120
- Somewhat, but honestly,
my grandfather, my parents,

00:17:44.120 --> 00:17:46.670
my dad raced also, they
never pushed it on us.

00:17:46.670 --> 00:17:48.882
I wanted to play middle
linebacker in football,

00:17:48.882 --> 00:17:49.962
that's what I wanted to do.

00:17:49.962 --> 00:17:50.795
- And then you realized, oh fuck.

00:17:50.795 --> 00:17:52.440
- I'm too small.

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:54.090
- What year was that,
fourth grade, seventh grade?

00:17:54.090 --> 00:17:57.560
- Junior PeeWee, I was going
to pee wee football and

00:17:57.560 --> 00:18:00.870
the hits were getting
harder, I was like 13.

00:18:00.870 --> 00:18:03.650
- You're like, why is
this guy running over me?

00:18:03.650 --> 00:18:06.050
- Yeah, I was like 13 and
I was fortunate enough to

00:18:06.050 --> 00:18:08.530
have a grandfather that
I could call and say

00:18:08.530 --> 00:18:11.850
hey, I wanna try racing,
and I'd seen kids our age

00:18:11.850 --> 00:18:13.680
running around a track at
Charlotte Motor Speedway,

00:18:13.680 --> 00:18:15.980
it's inside of the track,
it's a quarter mile.

00:18:17.560 --> 00:18:19.423
From that day, got in a
car for the first time,

00:18:19.423 --> 00:18:22.154
it's like whatever's in
his blood, my dad's blood,

00:18:22.154 --> 00:18:23.440
it clicked in.

00:18:23.440 --> 00:18:24.370
- Really.

00:18:24.370 --> 00:18:26.180
- I remember leaving the
track that day and I knew

00:18:26.180 --> 00:18:29.710
I loved what I just did
and my grandfather and my

00:18:29.710 --> 00:18:32.340
dad and mom all had a conversation
with my brother and I,

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:34.780
we started at the same time,
he was 15 and I was 13.

00:18:34.780 --> 00:18:37.920
I made the call, he might
say it, but I made the call

00:18:37.920 --> 00:18:39.058
to get us our first test.

00:18:39.058 --> 00:18:41.780
We left the track and they
said, If this is what you guys

00:18:41.780 --> 00:18:43.620
wanna do, you're gonna commit to it.

00:18:43.620 --> 00:18:45.883
If you really feel like you
wanna do this, we'll commit

00:18:45.883 --> 00:18:49.061
and we'll try to make it
happen and you guys can race

00:18:49.061 --> 00:18:52.277
as much as you want, but you're
going to commit to doing it

00:18:52.277 --> 00:18:53.210
and do it right.

00:18:53.210 --> 00:18:54.400
- You can't be half pregnant.

00:18:54.400 --> 00:18:58.710
- Yeah, exactly, and at
that time, at 13, I was like

00:18:58.710 --> 00:19:01.890
for sure, I'm in, I'm gonna
do it, I'm gonna be great,

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:04.910
and I committed, we
committed, and I ran 70 to 80

00:19:04.910 --> 00:19:08.877
races a year, leaving school
on Thursdays and Fridays,

00:19:08.877 --> 00:19:10.810
doing all my homework
Sunday night, getting home

00:19:10.810 --> 00:19:13.470
Sunday night, even when
I was 16 in high school,

00:19:13.470 --> 00:19:15.462
I would go off, my dad
would be at the race track

00:19:15.462 --> 00:19:18.550
helping our cup teams, I
would be racing a dirt car

00:19:18.550 --> 00:19:21.567
out in the midwest,
drive home, do homework,

00:19:21.567 --> 00:19:23.273
then once I got in high school

00:19:23.273 --> 00:19:24.830
I did more one on one classes.

00:19:24.830 --> 00:19:27.670
I really just committed
my life to racing and was

00:19:27.670 --> 00:19:29.370
successful all the way up through.

00:19:30.348 --> 00:19:32.808
And you get the haters that
say because it's your family,

00:19:32.808 --> 00:19:35.400
but I was able to be successful.

00:19:35.400 --> 00:19:38.130
You don't get to the Cup
Series just off of being

00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:40.003
helped the whole way, you don't last.

00:19:40.003 --> 00:19:41.590
But now I made it to the Cup Series,

00:19:41.590 --> 00:19:44.576
I won in the Truck Series, my
first year I won two races.

00:19:44.576 --> 00:19:47.946
I beat Kyle for my first
win, which was huge.

00:19:47.946 --> 00:19:49.542
I look up to his talent.

00:19:49.542 --> 00:19:51.694
He's our best driver
in the sport right now,

00:19:51.694 --> 00:19:53.118
I feel like talent wise.

00:19:53.118 --> 00:19:54.220
I beat him for my first win.

00:19:54.220 --> 00:19:57.123
- Who is this Truex guy
that I just found out about,

00:19:57.123 --> 00:19:58.739
who's from Jersey, he's
got like a wrestling name.

00:19:58.739 --> 00:20:00.850
That's literally what I was
thinking of, I mean, Truex.

00:20:00.850 --> 00:20:02.350
- He's tearing it up these last few years.

00:20:02.350 --> 00:20:04.110
- What's going on with these
three guys, it sounds like

00:20:04.110 --> 00:20:06.390
have won like almost every
fucking race this year?

00:20:06.390 --> 00:20:07.510
- They're dominating for sure.

00:20:07.510 --> 00:20:08.909
- Is everybody else pissed?

00:20:08.909 --> 00:20:10.617
- Yeah, pretty much.

00:20:10.617 --> 00:20:13.678
(laughing)

00:20:13.678 --> 00:20:17.450
So, one early in the Xfinity
Series, one in Indianapolis,

00:20:17.450 --> 00:20:21.130
was my first win there, to
get a win at the Brickyard

00:20:21.130 --> 00:20:23.074
was huge, just so much history.

00:20:23.074 --> 00:20:26.480
Now my second year in
Cup Series I'm not racing

00:20:26.480 --> 00:20:28.300
for my grandfather's team,
I'd raced for his team

00:20:28.300 --> 00:20:29.133
my whole career.

00:20:29.133 --> 00:20:30.443
- Why did that happen?

00:20:30.443 --> 00:20:34.180
- Just because I ran Xfinity
three years and he had

00:20:34.180 --> 00:20:37.564
drivers on contracts for his
jobs and sponsor obilgations

00:20:37.564 --> 00:20:39.383
and I felt like I was ready--

00:20:39.383 --> 00:20:40.216
- That's cool.

00:20:40.216 --> 00:20:42.300
- To make the next level,
and the team was actually--

00:20:42.300 --> 00:20:44.480
- That says a lot about
everybody in that story.

00:20:44.480 --> 00:20:47.930
- Yeah, it's been really awesome for me.

00:20:47.930 --> 00:20:49.810
The team is still a technical
alliance so we share

00:20:49.810 --> 00:20:52.600
information with my
grandfather's team, but we are

00:20:52.600 --> 00:20:54.630
our own separate entity still.

00:20:54.630 --> 00:20:56.350
All the cars come out of a different shop

00:20:56.350 --> 00:20:58.598
that's actually an hour away.

00:20:58.598 --> 00:21:01.870
So I've been racing for them
for two years and we had

00:21:01.870 --> 00:21:05.067
their most successful season
last year in my rookie season

00:21:05.067 --> 00:21:07.660
statistic wise across the
board and we're really trying

00:21:07.660 --> 00:21:08.833
to build a program.

00:21:09.940 --> 00:21:13.200
It's up and down, like you
said, going from Truck Series,

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:15.250
I won and was in my rookie
year was battling for a

00:21:15.250 --> 00:21:17.300
championship all the way
down to the last race

00:21:17.300 --> 00:21:20.310
and got wrecked by a good
friend now, but Kyle Larson,

00:21:20.310 --> 00:21:22.660
making his first or second
start in the Truck Series,

00:21:22.660 --> 00:21:25.280
he wrecked me and I lost the championship.

00:21:25.280 --> 00:21:26.360
- So you hated his face.

00:21:26.360 --> 00:21:27.193
- For a while.

00:21:27.193 --> 00:21:28.490
- Like two years?

00:21:28.490 --> 00:21:30.740
- Yeah, about two years,
and then I got to know him

00:21:30.740 --> 00:21:32.340
a little bit better.

00:21:32.340 --> 00:21:34.930
Same thing, Xfinity Series,
was battling for championships

00:21:34.930 --> 00:21:36.931
and now in the Cup Series
it's like taking three steps

00:21:36.931 --> 00:21:40.920
and it's so much tougher but
we're battling it, I feel like.

00:21:40.920 --> 00:21:42.379
- What's been your best results so far?

00:21:42.379 --> 00:21:45.813
- Sixth this year, the race
he won I finished sixth.

00:21:47.343 --> 00:21:50.170
I actually filled in for
Tony Stewart three years ago

00:21:50.170 --> 00:21:53.720
when he had a back injury and
finished sixth in his car.

00:21:53.720 --> 00:21:56.320
He started the race, he
got out, I got in the car

00:21:56.320 --> 00:21:57.495
and finished sixth.

00:21:57.495 --> 00:21:58.550
- Really, I didn't know that happened.

00:21:58.550 --> 00:21:59.383
- That doesn't count.

00:21:59.383 --> 00:22:01.360
- Unofficially, I didn't
get the finish for that.

00:22:01.360 --> 00:22:02.193
- He does?

00:22:02.193 --> 00:22:03.680
- He gets sixth place.

00:22:03.680 --> 00:22:05.570
- Really, the person that
starts, that's interesting.

00:22:05.570 --> 00:22:07.200
- I crossed the line, finished sixth.

00:22:07.200 --> 00:22:10.010
- I heard you loud and
clear, I understood.

00:22:10.010 --> 00:22:11.676
- I wish the statistics were.
- Yeah, I get it.

00:22:11.676 --> 00:22:16.070
- No but it's been an awesome
career, I've just been trying

00:22:16.070 --> 00:22:17.854
to make the most out of
this moment in my life.

00:22:17.854 --> 00:22:18.687
- With the vlogging and the show.

00:22:18.687 --> 00:22:23.068
- With everything, yeah, it
found me here today, which is,

00:22:23.068 --> 00:22:26.870
as you can tell, I'm so excited,
this is really cool for me.

00:22:26.870 --> 00:22:28.044
- I appreciate that, I'm flattered.

00:22:28.044 --> 00:22:29.894
Actually, let's make that transition.

00:22:31.450 --> 00:22:33.210
I know that Tyler got
ahold of you and your team

00:22:33.210 --> 00:22:34.633
and so I'm glad you're here.

00:22:35.602 --> 00:22:38.470
For you Charly, how do you
think about social, Instagram,

00:22:38.470 --> 00:22:40.780
what do you use, what do you
like, how much of an impact

00:22:40.780 --> 00:22:43.360
does it have, what's good
about it, what's bad about it?

00:22:43.360 --> 00:22:45.940
- I mean, there's such a wide spectrum

00:22:45.940 --> 00:22:48.690
from what's good about it
to what's terrible about it,

00:22:48.690 --> 00:22:50.047
in my opinion, but it's
one of those things

00:22:50.047 --> 00:22:53.079
that's not going anywhere,
it's getting bigger and bigger

00:22:53.079 --> 00:22:55.900
as the days go by so I'm
just trying to embrace it

00:22:55.900 --> 00:22:56.950
as much as I can.

00:22:56.950 --> 00:22:58.650
I mean, as far as building--

00:22:58.650 --> 00:23:01.744
- Take me back to Type A you,
we don't drink we don't drink.

00:23:01.744 --> 00:23:05.730
Were you a Facebook girl back then?

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:08.960
What years, you know, what
did you use back then,

00:23:08.960 --> 00:23:10.407
anything, nothing?
- Well, yeah I mean I was--

00:23:10.407 --> 00:23:13.000
- Did you grow up on MySpace as a kid?

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:14.460
- MySpace, I had the top eight.

00:23:14.460 --> 00:23:16.060
- Were you strategic about your top eight?

00:23:16.060 --> 00:23:19.140
- Oh absolutely, you did something wrong,

00:23:19.140 --> 00:23:19.980
you were outta there.

00:23:19.980 --> 00:23:22.280
- When you would drop somebody, like Carol

00:23:22.280 --> 00:23:24.760
from science class, from your
top eight, and you came to

00:23:24.760 --> 00:23:25.990
school that day, because I was an old man

00:23:25.990 --> 00:23:28.035
by the time MySpace came.
- Yeah, it made a difference.

00:23:28.035 --> 00:23:28.868
- For you youngsters.

00:23:28.868 --> 00:23:29.820
- [Charly] Avoid eye contact.

00:23:29.820 --> 00:23:30.800
- Was that a real thing?

00:23:30.800 --> 00:23:33.100
- Oh yeah, absolutely,

00:23:33.100 --> 00:23:36.459
it was very much like disowning a friend.

00:23:36.459 --> 00:23:37.292
- I think somebody needs to bring back

00:23:37.292 --> 00:23:38.600
the top eight dynamic.

00:23:38.600 --> 00:23:41.936
I've actually been having
this weird feeling,

00:23:41.936 --> 00:23:44.670
this just not a dream,
not a daydream, but this

00:23:44.670 --> 00:23:46.720
overall feeling that we're on the,

00:23:46.720 --> 00:23:49.100
I feel like there's these
two girls somewhere like

00:23:49.100 --> 00:23:52.010
in the suburbs of Denver
working on an app.

00:23:52.010 --> 00:23:54.050
I can feel it, you know, like the force,

00:23:54.050 --> 00:23:55.230
I feel like it's Star Wars.

00:23:55.230 --> 00:23:57.950
I feel like I'm so in tune
with the social media zeitgeist

00:23:57.950 --> 00:24:00.900
that I think we're on the
verge of something coming out.

00:24:00.900 --> 00:24:03.590
And now I'm going to try
to use my powers to make

00:24:03.590 --> 00:24:06.465
those kids decide to make it
more about top eight dynamics.

00:24:06.465 --> 00:24:07.622
- We can't offend anybody any more.

00:24:07.622 --> 00:24:09.403
- Do we want that now,
everyone's so sensitive.

00:24:09.403 --> 00:24:11.630
- Is that where you're going with this?

00:24:11.630 --> 00:24:12.613
- I'm just saying--

00:24:12.613 --> 00:24:15.464
- I'm not going to go
overly political, but you

00:24:15.464 --> 00:24:20.464
believe even in this short
10, 12, 15 year window,

00:24:21.070 --> 00:24:24.030
that the top eight dynamic
would not play in the current

00:24:24.030 --> 00:24:25.710
state of like people
offended about everything,

00:24:25.710 --> 00:24:26.730
about everything about everything.

00:24:26.730 --> 00:24:27.563
- Yeah.

00:24:27.563 --> 00:24:29.188
- That's interesting.

00:24:29.188 --> 00:24:33.960
I think the country's very
split now on the defending.

00:24:33.960 --> 00:24:37.170
To me, I'm unbelievably
socially liberal and I think

00:24:37.170 --> 00:24:39.330
everybody should love
everybody, but I will say,

00:24:39.330 --> 00:24:43.560
like eighth place trophies
is the bane of my existence.

00:24:43.560 --> 00:24:46.340
The think that I've loved about,
the thing I've loved so far

00:24:46.340 --> 00:24:48.480
talking about all of this
energy of competitiveness

00:24:48.480 --> 00:24:51.440
and things of that nature,
is this, I don't understand

00:24:51.440 --> 00:24:53.220
how people want to put
their kids in situations

00:24:53.220 --> 00:24:54.940
where there aren't winning and losing.

00:24:54.940 --> 00:24:57.860
You fundamentally don't
set them up for success.

00:24:57.860 --> 00:24:59.070
They're like, finished.

00:24:59.070 --> 00:25:00.500
- Yeah, my dad never let me win.

00:25:00.500 --> 00:25:01.333
- Anything.

00:25:01.333 --> 00:25:02.246
- No.

00:25:02.246 --> 00:25:05.910
- I'm like destroying my six
year old son in everything.

00:25:05.910 --> 00:25:08.460
The whole family is bent
out of shape and I'm like,

00:25:09.530 --> 00:25:11.320
he's going to be a loser otherwise.

00:25:11.320 --> 00:25:12.153
I genuinely believe that.

00:25:12.153 --> 00:25:13.236
- Yeah, remember there was

00:25:13.236 --> 00:25:16.690
that big debacle over
the participation awards.

00:25:16.690 --> 00:25:19.230
Remember, when was that, a few years ago?

00:25:19.230 --> 00:25:21.560
- Good news, I've had
that as a macro thesis

00:25:21.560 --> 00:25:23.620
for the last 20 years.

00:25:23.620 --> 00:25:25.830
I also think school does that a lot.

00:25:25.830 --> 00:25:28.476
I actually think, back to being
Type A, like student life,

00:25:28.476 --> 00:25:32.690
there's been this enormous
push on being an entrepreneur,

00:25:32.690 --> 00:25:37.160
which I'm very in, and I
failed in school for a reason.

00:25:37.160 --> 00:25:38.820
Winning in school is an incredible skill.

00:25:38.820 --> 00:25:41.110
You figure out the system and you play it.

00:25:41.110 --> 00:25:44.293
The problem is, much like the
shift from truck to the cup,

00:25:45.490 --> 00:25:49.600
being an entrepreneur is
literally like swimming

00:25:49.600 --> 00:25:52.010
versus cooking, to being good at school.

00:25:52.010 --> 00:25:53.830
They're completely different.

00:25:53.830 --> 00:25:56.770
I think that fake environments,
is what I call them,

00:25:56.770 --> 00:25:58.683
are very very very dangerous.

00:26:00.380 --> 00:26:02.710
I mean, as a matter of fact,
you're probably successful

00:26:02.710 --> 00:26:05.240
because your family over
corrected by not putting you

00:26:05.240 --> 00:26:07.990
in a fake environment
because of the lineage,

00:26:07.990 --> 00:26:10.140
and the last name, like that's
the only way you could be

00:26:10.140 --> 00:26:12.805
this happy and successful.

00:26:12.805 --> 00:26:17.250
- Yeah, I almost over corrected,
I was working on my car.

00:26:17.250 --> 00:26:20.931
At certain levels I had smaller
teams than other people.

00:26:20.931 --> 00:26:22.762
- You had to, you had to, you had to.

00:26:22.762 --> 00:26:25.157
- And I probably had more
backing in capability growing up.

00:26:25.157 --> 00:26:26.320
- Because this dude coming up who came out

00:26:26.320 --> 00:26:27.450
of left field from a different place

00:26:27.450 --> 00:26:28.660
with not that background,

00:26:28.660 --> 00:26:30.170
you can't help it as a human, you're

00:26:30.170 --> 00:26:33.777
going to deploy that
cynicism, and you're going to,

00:26:35.668 --> 00:26:37.390
if you're the outsider not
with it, that's the chip.

00:26:37.390 --> 00:26:39.600
And if you're in it you've
got the reverse is the chip.

00:26:39.600 --> 00:26:40.800
- [Ty] It's definitely the reverse.

00:26:40.800 --> 00:26:41.960
- 100%

00:26:41.960 --> 00:26:44.794
- Then you wanna shove it
in everybody's face when--

00:26:44.794 --> 00:26:47.100
- Like, the I told you so,

00:26:47.100 --> 00:26:50.153
is the most fucking delicious flavor ever.

00:26:51.100 --> 00:26:53.643
I'm obsessed with it. Do
you feel like that now?

00:26:53.643 --> 00:26:56.278
Like you've clearly, you're
on a pretty big stage now

00:26:56.278 --> 00:26:58.320
you've been talking shit your whole life

00:26:58.320 --> 00:27:00.157
with your friends, like
I'm gonna be on TV,

00:27:00.157 --> 00:27:03.410
like clearly some of
them smiled in your face

00:27:03.410 --> 00:27:04.790
but didn't think so.

00:27:04.790 --> 00:27:07.990
Do you love coming back to Indy
like fuck you mother fucker?

00:27:07.990 --> 00:27:10.650
- You know what, I
personally don't because

00:27:10.650 --> 00:27:14.070
I always knew I was gonna be successful.

00:27:14.070 --> 00:27:16.330
- And you didn't give
a fuck what Karen said.

00:27:16.330 --> 00:27:17.900
- No, I didn't care
about Karen, fuck Karen.

00:27:17.900 --> 00:27:18.935
- You hate Karen.

00:27:18.935 --> 00:27:20.830
(laughing)

00:27:20.830 --> 00:27:22.580
- Yeah, I always knew I
was going to be successful

00:27:22.580 --> 00:27:24.251
whatever path I took.

00:27:24.251 --> 00:27:25.084
- Why?

00:27:25.084 --> 00:27:28.120
- I just don't accept failure.

00:27:28.120 --> 00:27:29.824
I'm telling you, when I say I'm Type A,

00:27:29.824 --> 00:27:33.340
it's a beautiful thing because it makes it

00:27:33.340 --> 00:27:35.180
so that I will never
accept failure as even

00:27:35.180 --> 00:27:36.940
a remote possibility.

00:27:36.940 --> 00:27:39.080
- How do you make, what's the fine line

00:27:39.080 --> 00:27:41.163
between determination and delusion?

00:27:43.530 --> 00:27:48.030
- I don't know, I think
if you, I don't think

00:27:49.660 --> 00:27:50.511
you can be delusional.

00:27:50.511 --> 00:27:52.908
I mean, if you're willing
to put the work in--

00:27:52.908 --> 00:27:54.270
- Well if you've ever
watched American Idol,

00:27:54.270 --> 00:27:58.890
but here's what happens, "Hi
Simon, I'm the next Beyonce."

00:27:58.890 --> 00:28:00.530
Wow, very exciting, please sing.

00:28:00.530 --> 00:28:02.570
Bah, that's delusion.

00:28:02.570 --> 00:28:03.570
- Okay, right, right.

00:28:03.570 --> 00:28:06.510
- Like these guys came
up with guys driving,

00:28:06.510 --> 00:28:09.040
their homies at 16, they're
fucking stealing a beer

00:28:09.040 --> 00:28:11.700
from 7-11 after a race,
and Rick looks at them like

00:28:11.700 --> 00:28:13.240
motherfucker I'm going
to be there one day,

00:28:13.240 --> 00:28:15.320
and they're, through
their heads, they're like,

00:28:15.320 --> 00:28:18.200
I know this, you have no shot.

00:28:18.200 --> 00:28:19.033
That's delusion.

00:28:19.033 --> 00:28:21.240
- True, true, I stand corrected.

00:28:21.240 --> 00:28:22.710
- And by the way, I'll be honest with you,

00:28:22.710 --> 00:28:24.180
it was funny to hear
you pause a little bit,

00:28:24.180 --> 00:28:25.990
I was literally laughing
really fast in my head.

00:28:25.990 --> 00:28:28.540
I'm like, I'm glad nobody's
ever asked me that question

00:28:28.540 --> 00:28:29.973
because I don't know either.

00:28:30.850 --> 00:28:32.280
It's super hard to think about.

00:28:32.280 --> 00:28:34.830
It seems like an incredible fine line.

00:28:34.830 --> 00:28:37.300
- It's intuition, I feel
like it's more intuition

00:28:37.300 --> 00:28:40.170
whether you know you can
keep going and you know

00:28:40.170 --> 00:28:41.160
that you don't want to give up

00:28:41.160 --> 00:28:43.070
because you know you've got it.

00:28:43.070 --> 00:28:44.700
- It's like being honest with yourself.

00:28:44.700 --> 00:28:48.140
- I mean, it's delusional
for me to say I'm going to go

00:28:48.140 --> 00:28:50.130
on American Idol and even
have a chance at winning

00:28:50.130 --> 00:28:52.702
because I am an awful
singer, I am the worst.

00:28:52.702 --> 00:28:54.552
- I'm really bad too.

00:28:54.552 --> 00:28:56.197
- I'm a great singer.
- What?

00:28:56.197 --> 00:28:57.735
- I'm a great singer.
- Seriously?

00:28:57.735 --> 00:28:58.590
- Yeah.

00:28:58.590 --> 00:29:00.988
- No, on a very serious note,
because you've kind of got

00:29:00.988 --> 00:29:03.615
the joke face right now,
do you think you can

00:29:03.615 --> 00:29:05.300
sing a little bit?

00:29:05.300 --> 00:29:06.133
- No.

00:29:06.133 --> 00:29:08.301
- Yeah, I saw the joke face.

00:29:08.301 --> 00:29:09.273
- I do karaoke, I love karaoke.

00:29:10.960 --> 00:29:12.326
- What's your go-to karaoke song?

00:29:12.326 --> 00:29:13.910
- Should Have Been A Cowboy.

00:29:13.910 --> 00:29:17.016
- That's exactly what I
thought you were going to say.

00:29:17.016 --> 00:29:18.870
- I love Toby Keith.

00:29:18.870 --> 00:29:22.075
- But if you have a skill set
that you know you're good at

00:29:22.075 --> 00:29:24.723
and you push in that direction.

00:29:24.723 --> 00:29:26.410
- Do you have siblings?

00:29:26.410 --> 00:29:27.270
- No.

00:29:27.270 --> 00:29:30.180
- Wow, and what kind of
mom and dad situation

00:29:30.180 --> 00:29:33.130
did you have, the best?

00:29:33.130 --> 00:29:35.930
- I mean, yes, my mother,
still to this day,

00:29:35.930 --> 00:29:38.180
she calls me so many times a day.

00:29:38.180 --> 00:29:40.990
I'm like, Mom, we've already
talked three times today,

00:29:40.990 --> 00:29:43.510
I love you but there's
nothing left to say today,

00:29:43.510 --> 00:29:45.593
let's shut this down.

00:29:46.580 --> 00:29:49.000
My mother was always the doting,

00:29:49.000 --> 00:29:51.510
like everything I did was perfect.

00:29:51.510 --> 00:29:52.460
- She was the cheerleader.

00:29:52.460 --> 00:29:54.180
- Yes
- Okay, Dad?

00:29:54.180 --> 00:29:56.480
- Dad was the opposite and still is,

00:29:56.480 --> 00:29:57.973
like he will not tell
me what I want to hear.

00:29:57.973 --> 00:30:00.340
- It's interesting, and I
had this, do you think the

00:30:00.340 --> 00:30:04.008
polar opposites of how they
did it, in that pulling,

00:30:04.008 --> 00:30:06.490
you were able to develop the strength,

00:30:06.490 --> 00:30:08.350
make the diamonds from that pressure?

00:30:08.350 --> 00:30:09.825
- Yeah, I think so because--

00:30:09.825 --> 00:30:10.658
- Because you had both behaviors.

00:30:10.658 --> 00:30:12.790
- If you have just one
I think it can push you

